@node What It Adds
@section What It Adds

Compared to Nael 0.8.3:

@itemize
@item
An infoview (@code{lena-infoview}, @samp{C-c C-i}) that talks to Lean's RPC: goals and the
expected type, with the changes of the tactic at point, folding and filters;
subterms described by ElDoc, tooltips and the help buffer @samp{*Lena Help*}, and
followed to their definitions; the messages of the line of point, with traces,
suggestions to apply and links to the explanations of errors. Files:
@code{lena-rpc.el}, @code{lena-render.el}, @code{lena-infoview.el}, @code{lena-infoview-buffer.el},
@code{lena-infoview-hover.el}.
@item
Signs in the left margin for diagnostics, complete proofs, unsolved goals,
uses of @samp{sorry} and lines being processed (@code{lena-margin-mode}, @code{lena-margin.el}).
@item
For Eglot: restarting the current file, which builds its imports once
(@code{lena-eglot-restart-file}, @samp{C-c C-r}); not building imports when a file is opened
(@code{lena-eglot-build-dependencies}); Lean's silent diagnostics and widgets in
messages.
@item
Keys for @uref{https://github.com/emacs-evil/evil, Evil}, in the separate package @code{lena-evil}, and a Doom Emacs module (@samp{doom/lang/lena/}).
@item
Smaller changes: a better syntax table, also for ElDoc, and macros recognized
as such; the first goal is no longer echoed (@code{lena-eglot-echo-goal}); a shorter
ElDoc delay in Lena buffers (@code{lena-eldoc-idle-delay}).
@item
Tests in @samp{test/}: @samp{emacs -Q --batch -l build.el test}, and @samp{test-live} with a Lean
installation.
@item
Lena needs Emacs 30.1, instead of 29.1.
@item
Lena drops Nael's support for lsp-mode (@code{nael-lsp}) and uses Eglot only.
@end itemize

@node Get Started
@chapter Get Started

Lena is not on Elpa or Melpa yet. To install Lena, use @code{package-vc} or a clone of
this repository, see the @emph{Installation} chapter below.

Lena, (upstream) Nael and Lean4-Mode each make themselves the major mode for @samp{.lean}
files, so install only one of them.

@node Usage
@chapter Usage

Language Server Protocol (LSP):

@itemize
@item
@samp{C-c C-e} (@code{eglot}) starts Eglot, the LSP client.
@item
@samp{C-c C-r} (@code{lena-eglot-restart-file}) makes the Lean server reprocess the current
file, building its imports first if they are out of date. Opening a file does
not build its imports, unless @code{lena-eglot-build-dependencies} is non-nil.
@item
@samp{C-c C-i} (@code{lena-infoview}) toggles the infoview, a window that shows the goals
and the expected type at point and follows point. It needs Lean 4.30 or
later.
@item
@samp{C-h .} (@code{eldoc-doc-buffer}) displays the ElDoc documentation buffer showing
tactic state and expected goal at point, unless the infoview shows them.
@end itemize

Signs in the left margin, while Eglot manages the buffer (@code{lena-margin-mode}):

@itemize
@item
@samp{‼} marks the line of an error, @samp{!} that of a warning or a note. A diagnostic that
spans several lines gets a bar over them instead, @samp{┌}, @samp{│}, @samp{└}, in its color. These
are the indicators that Flymake shows otherwise; Flymake still underlines the
diagnostics and shows their messages. The tooltip of a sign shows the message,
and clicking it lists all diagnostics.
@item
@samp{✓} marks a proof that is complete, @samp{✗} the last line of one with unsolved
goals, where it goes on, and @samp{⚠} a declaration that uses @samp{sorry}. Other signs,
such as emoji, can be set with @code{lena-margin-goals-accomplished},
@code{lena-margin-unsolved-goals} and @code{lena-margin-admitted}; with most fonts, an emoji
makes its line taller, unless @code{lena-margin-emoji-height} makes it smaller, such
as 0.7.
@item
An orange @samp{│} marks the lines that Lean is still processing.
@end itemize

Infoview:

@itemize
@item
Inspired by lean.nvim, in a complete proof, a line above the goals says "Goals
accomplished ✓", and replaces "no goals" when none are left.
@item
Below the goals and the expected type, the section @emph{Messages} shows Lean's
messages on the line of point: errors, warnings, and output such as that of
@samp{#check}. Messages that start on another line, like an error about a whole
proof, are folded; messages about the whole file, like that its imports are
out of date, are not, and come first. That message has a @samp{[restart file]}
button, which does what @samp{C-c C-r} does. Their terms are subterms like those of
goals. @samp{RET} or @samp{mouse-1} on the position of a message goes there in the source.
@item
Traces, as with @samp{set_option trace.Meta.synthInstance true in}, are headings: @samp{TAB}
on one shows its children, which Lean sends on request only.
@item
Suggestions such as Lean's "Try this" have an @samp{[apply]} button that replaces the
code in the source with the suggestion; this includes the tactics of libraries
that suggest through Lean's @samp{TryThis}. Lean's hints show the change they suggest
as a diff, which applies the same way. The code of an error, such as
@samp{[lean.invalidField]}, opens its explanation in Lean's reference manual.
@item
@samp{v} (@code{lena-infoview-view-options}) opens a menu for customizing what the infoview
shows: which hypotheses to hide, whether to show the target first and the
changes of the tactic at point, and how to show the expected type.

@item
With @uref{https://github.com/emacs-evil/evil, Evil} and the @code{lena-evil} package, @samp{(with-eval-after-load 'evil (lena-evil-setup))}
binds keys in normal state that don't clash with its motions: @samp{]g} and @samp{[g} for
the next and previous goal, @samp{]h} and @samp{[h} for headings, @samp{gr} to refresh, @samp{K} to
describe the subterm at point, and, with a local leader, @samp{<localleader> v} for
the view options and @samp{<localleader> y} to copy the goal.
@end itemize

Infoview, on a subterm of a goal:

@itemize
@item
ElDoc echoes the subterm with all its arguments made explicit, and its
type. With @uref{https://github.com/casouri/eldoc-box, eldoc-box}, this appears in a popup.
@item
With @code{tooltip-mode}, the tooltip of the subterm under the mouse shows it with
its type and documentation. We suggest turning native tooltips off for better
formatting, by setting @code{use-system-tooltips} to @samp{nil}.
@item
@samp{RET}, @samp{mouse-1} or @samp{C-h .} (@code{lena-infoview-describe}) describes the subterm in the
help buffer @samp{*Lena Help*}: its explicit form, its type, its documentation, and
how the tactic at point changes it. Its subterms can be described in turn, and
the history goes back and forth: @samp{l} and @samp{r}, or @samp{C-o} and @samp{C-i} with Evil. In Doom
Emacs, @samp{K} describes too. Closing the help window returns to the infoview.
@item
@samp{M-.} (@code{xref-find-definitions}) shows the definition of the subterm in another
window, and @samp{M-,} (@code{xref-go-back}) returns to the infoview. In Doom Emacs, these
are @samp{gd} and @samp{C-o}.
@end itemize

Abbreviations:

@itemize
@item
@samp{C-c C-a} (@code{abbrev-mode}) enables abbreviations.
@item
@samp{C-c C-k} (@code{lena-abbrev-help}) echoes abbreviations that expand to the character
at point, or, if active, to the selected region.
@item
@samp{C-x '} (@code{expand-abbrev}) expands the abbreviation before point without
inserting any character. (Usually, you'd insert a space or newline to expand.)
@end itemize

Build:

@itemize
@item
@samp{C-c C-c} (@code{project-compile}) prompts for a command to build the current
project. It will suggest @samp{lake build} because Lena configures the
@code{compile-command} variable.
@end itemize

Navigation:

@itemize
@item
@samp{M-a} (@code{forward-sentence}) go to beginning of current sentence/block, or to
previous one.
@item
@samp{M-e} (@code{backward-sentence}) go to end of current sentence/block, or to next one.
@item
@samp{M-g i} (@code{imenu}) jumps to an interactively chosen definition in current buffer.
@end itemize

Comments:

@itemize
@item
@samp{M-;} (@code{comment-dwim}) toggles the active region as comment or, if no region is
active, creates a comment at end of line.
@item
@samp{M-q} (@code{prog-fill-reindent-defun}) fills the string- or comment-paragraph at
point, or reindents the point-surrounding code.
@end itemize

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration

To view the complete list of Lena's user option variables from within Emacs,
customize the group @code{lena} (and its child-groups) with @samp{M-x customize-group RET
lena RET}. This chapter highlights and elaborates on some of them.

As an exception, Emacs customization groups cannot include keymaps. You can bind
custom keys in @code{lena-mode-map} like this:

@lisp
(keymap-set lena-mode-map "C-c ." #'highlight-symbol-at-point)
@end lisp

To automatically enable features (e.g. abbreviations or Eglot) when Lena is
started, customize @code{lena-mode-hook} as follows:

@lisp
(add-hook 'lena-mode-hook #'abbrev-mode)
(add-hook 'lena-mode-hook #'eglot-ensure)
@end lisp

The signs in the margin can be changed, also for a single project in its
@samp{.dir-locals.el}. For example, with chickens:

@lisp
(setq lena-margin-goals-accomplished "🐣"  ; complete proof
      lena-margin-unsolved-goals "🥚"      ; unsolved goals
      lena-margin-admitted "🍗"            ; uses sorry
      lena-margin-emoji-height 0.7)        ; as tall as text lines
@end lisp

The margin is as wide as its widest sign.

To keep Flymake's indicators instead of the signs, set @code{lena-margin} to @samp{nil} before
opening Lean files.

For the infoview to show messages with their terms, traces and suggestions, Lena
asks Lean for them when the project's @samp{lean-toolchain} names Lean 4.30 or
later. Then the text of a trace that Flymake shows is only "(trace)", as in VS
Code. To keep the full text in Flymake instead, set @code{lena-eglot-widgets} to @samp{nil}
before Lean starts.

@strong{The following behavior will likely change soon}: Lena's Abbrev tables use the
@samp{:regexp} property which is @uref{https://github.com/minad/cape/discussions/158#discussioncomment-14687758, incompatible} with @uref{https://github.com/company-mode/company-mode, Company}'s @code{company-abbrev} and @uref{https://github.com/minad/cape, Cape}'s
@code{cape-abbrev}. Instead, Lena supports completion-at-point natively via
@code{lena-abbrev-capf}. This CAPF is buffer-locally added to
@code{completion-at-point-functions} whenever Abbrev mode is enabled inside Lena. To
ditch this feature, set the @code{lena-abbrev-capf} variable to @samp{nil}.

