@node Manual Installation
@subsection Manual Installation

First clone the Git repository, then, in your Emacs initialization file, add
Lena to @code{load-path} and load it. For lazy loading the autoloads-file needs to be
built beforehand.

@lisp
;; git clone https://github.com/ISTA-PLV/nael.git ~/path/to/lena

(add-to-list 'load-path "~/path/to/lena/lena")
;; For Evil:
;; (add-to-list 'load-path "~/path/to/lena/lena-evil")

;; Option 1, eager loading:
(require 'lena)
;; (require 'lena-evil)

;; Option 2, lazy loading:
;; cd ~/path/to/lena && ./build.el autoloads
;; (require 'lena-autoloads)
;; (require 'lena-evil-autoloads)

;; Optionally, make readme available as info manual:
(require 'info)
(info-initialize)
(add-to-list 'Info-directory-list "~/path/to/lena/lena")
@end lisp

@node Installation via @code{package-vc}
@subsection Installation via @code{package-vc}

Since version 29, Emacs ships with @code{package-vc} which enables the built-in
package manager @code{package} to install from version-controlled source
repositories. Keep in mind to regularly upgrade such packages with the command
@code{package-vc-upgrade} or @code{package-vc-upgrade-all}. (And please ignore the warnings
that arise when @code{package-vc} attempts to byte-compile all Elisp source files in
the repository instead of just the package-specific ones.)

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Installation via @code{package-vc-selected-packages}}Installation via @code{package-vc-selected-packages}


In your Emacs session, select packages per @code{package-vc-selected-packages}
variable and install them with @samp{M-x package-vc-install-selected-packages
RET}. Persist the package selection in your Emacs initialization file
too. @code{package-vc} will generate the autoloads-file automatically. To lazy-load
Lena, load the autoloads-file.

@lisp
(require 'package-vc)

(setopt package-vc-selected-packages
        '((lena
           :doc "lena/README.org"
           :lisp-dir "lena"
           :url "https://github.com/ISTA-PLV/nael.git")
          ;; For Evil:
          ;; (lena-evil
          ;;  :lisp-dir "lena-evil"
          ;;  :url "https://github.com/ISTA-PLV/nael.git")
          ))

(package-initialize)

;; Option 1, lazy loading:
(require 'lena-autoloads)
;; (require 'lena-evil-autoloads)

;; Option 2, eager loading:
;; (require 'lena)
;; (require 'lena-evil)
@end lisp

@item
@anchor{Installation via @code{use-package} @samp{vc}}Installation via @code{use-package} @samp{:vc}


Since version 30 from 2025, Emacs' popular @code{use-package} macro supports a @samp{:vc}
keyword as interface to @code{package-vc}. (Because each package has autoload-cookies
on relevant expressions, and because @code{use-package} will load the autoloads-file
that is generated at installation, you don't need to specify load-triggers like
@samp{:mode}. I.e. lazy-loading should work out of the box.)

@lisp
(use-package lena
  :vc ( :doc "lena/README.org"
        :lisp-dir "lena"
        :url "https://github.com/ISTA-PLV/nael.git"))

;; For Evil:
;; (use-package lena-evil
;;   :after (lena evil)
;;   :vc ( :lisp-dir "lena-evil"
;;         :url "https://github.com/ISTA-PLV/nael.git")
;;   :config (lena-evil-setup))
@end lisp
@end enumerate

@node Installation in Doom Emacs
@section Installation in Doom Emacs

The repository contains a Doom Emacs module, @samp{:lang lena}, in @samp{doom/lang/lena/}. It
installs Lena and sets up its infoview, with keys under @samp{SPC m} and for Evil, and
replaces Doom's @samp{:lang lean} module, which uses upstream Nael. See its @samp{README.org}
for how to install it.

